<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    ==============================================================================================
    Directory.Build.props
    The single shared build contract for every project in the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.
    ==============================================================================================

    WHAT THIS FILE IS
    MSBuild imports this file automatically into every project beneath it by directory walk up, so
    it is the one place the build contract is expressed. Every project in PowerFramework.slnx
    inherits it, and the roster adds up in place so a wrong count here cannot survive a reading: the
    six shared libraries under shared/ (Kernel, Diagnostics, Eventful, Localization, Containers and
    Contracts), their six sibling test projects, the four service applications under services/, and
    the four service test projects - that is 6 + 6 + 4 + 4 = 20 projects, of which ten are test
    projects, matching the twenty project entries in the root PowerFramework.slnx and the twenty
    *.csproj files under shared/ and services/. No project restates any setting from here.

    EXACTLY ONE COPY MAY EXIST, AND IT LIVES HERE AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT
    A project under services/ therefore receives identical settings with zero duplication, which is
    what lets each service restore, build and test independently from a clean checkout while being
    unable to drift from the other three. Do NOT create a per service or per tree copy: MSBuild
    stops walking up at the FIRST Directory.Build.props it finds, so a second copy under services/
    or shared/ would silently shadow this one and reintroduce precisely the drift this file exists
    to prevent. A copy under a single service directory is the most damaging variant of that
    mistake, because the other three services would keep passing and nothing would report it.

    ORDER OF AUTHORSHIP, AND WHY IT MATTERS
        global.json                selects the SDK that evaluates this file
        Directory.Build.props      this file, which turns central package management ON
        Directory.Packages.props   inert until this file does so, then supplies every version

    TOOLCHAIN THIS CONTRACT IS VALIDATED AGAINST
        .NET SDK   whatever global.json at this same repository root pins. The version is NOT repeated
                   here: this comment carried 10.0.302 after that pin had moved to 10.0.303, which is
                   the failure mode a second copy of a number always eventually reaches. global.json
                   is the single authority, and it is two files away.
        Runtimes   Microsoft.NETCore.App and Microsoft.AspNetCore.App at the version
                   Directory.Packages.props pins for the framework reference packages, for the same
                   reason. Read it there.
        Language   C# 14, which is what net10.0 selects by default and is not set anywhere in this file

    THESE THREE LINES ARE A CLAIM ABOUT THE PRESENT AND ARE KEPT TRUE BY RE MEASUREMENT
    An earlier revision named SDK 10.0.302 with 10.0.10 runtimes while global.json beside this file
    pinned a later SDK and Directory.Packages.props pinned later platform packages, so the header
    contradicted its own repository. This block therefore names no version at all and moves whenever
    global.json does. The runtime pair is the one a framework dependent net10.0 process actually
    resolves, not merely one that is installed: a host can carry several patches side by side and roll
    forward to the newest, which is what a probe on this host reported.

    AND EVERY BEHAVIOURAL MEASUREMENT FURTHER DOWN THIS FILE NOW NAMES BOTH SDKS IT HOLDS ON.
    Three comments below record MSBuild behaviours that were reproduced rather than reasoned about, and
    each named only SDK 10.0.302 - the SDK pinned when the measurement was taken. That reads to a later
    reader as a claim about a toolchain the repository no longer pins, and it leaves the obvious
    question - does the behaviour still hold? - unanswered by the file that depends on the answer.
    Rather than delete the provenance or restate it as timeless, each was RE MEASURED on the SDK
    global.json pins today and now names both. The three re measurements, all on SDK 10.0.303:

      * Configuration propagation. `dotnet build -c Release` in services/gateway-service put every
        referenced shared project in bin/Release; the same command with the property overridden to
        true on the command line put four of them in bin/Debug and failed with PFW0001.
      * The artifacts layout pivot. The same build directed at an artifacts path produced the pivot
        directory 'release' in lower case, and PFW0001 selected nothing, so the lowercased comparison
        below is still what makes both layouts pass.
      * Both layouts, warning clean. Zero warnings and zero errors under each.

    A future SDK bump repeats those three commands and adds its version here. That is cheap, and it is
    the only way a comment claiming a measurement stays worth reading.

    THE PATCH LEVEL ITSELF IS A DECLARED DEVIATION, RECORDED AWAY FROM HERE ON PURPOSE. Agent Action
    Plan 0.5.1 names SDK 10.0.302 and 10.0.10 runtimes; the repository pins later ones, because the
    10.0.11 platform release fixes ten CVEs against 10.0.10 and holding the plan's figure would ship a
    knowingly vulnerable platform. Directory.Packages.props carries that reasoning where the six
    platform pins are, and docs/BUILD.md 1.6.2 D9 carries it as a numbered deviation from the frozen
    plan. This comment deliberately does not restate the reasoning: one authority per fact is the whole
    point of the paragraph above it.

    PROVENANCE: THERE IS NO LEGACY SOURCE EQUIVALENT TO TRANSLATE
    The Appeon PowerBuilder 2021 framework this .NET tree is being added to has no build system in
    the MSBuild sense. Its nearest analogues are the two PowerBuilder project objects, and nothing
    here is a translation of either, because neither is usable:

        ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj   29 PBD: entries, COM:Appeon,       CPY: empty
        ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/p_pfw.srj     28 PBD: entries, COM:Sybase, Inc., CPY: empty

    They disagree on library count, they disagree on whether pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl participates at
    all, they disagree on the vendor, p_pfw.srj embeds developer workstation absolute paths under
    F:\pfw\ together with the retired library name pfwUtils.pbl, and BOTH declare
    pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which does not exist anywhere in the repository. Neither would build
    as written, so both are read as REFERENCE for build INTENT only. Their empty CPY: field, and
    their COM: values naming the PowerBuilder IDE vendor rather than the framework author, are also
    why the assembly metadata group below takes its holder from the root LICENSE instead.

    THE LEGACY TREE IS READ ONLY AND SHARES THIS WORKING DIRECTORY
    ws_objects/, oldversion/125/, pack/, res/, samples/, sciter_control/, the pre-existing browser
    harness directories under tests/, every *.pbl, *.pbt, *.pbw, *.pbr and *.pbd, and the closed
    source native Windows *.dll binaries all sit in this same checkout. They are the behavioural
    oracle for parity testing and are never a build input. This file therefore declares NO ItemGroup
    of any kind, so nothing here can pull a legacy path into a build, and the C# projects that
    inherit these settings live only under shared/ and services/, where the SDK's default source
    globbing cannot reach any legacy path.
  -->

  <!--
    FRAMEWORK AND LANGUAGE
    net10.0 is the validated target framework; nothing in this repository multi targets, so the
    singular TargetFramework is correct rather than TargetFrameworks.

    LangVersion latest resolves to C# 14 on this SDK. It is safe to leave floating precisely because
    global.json pins the SDK with rollForward latestFeature, which confines roll forward to 10.0
    feature bands and so cannot silently cross a major language version.

    Nullable and ImplicitUsings are set unconditionally here, applications and test projects alike.
    Nullable reference types are part of the enterprise baseline for this refactor and are not a per
    project preference: the ported return code algebra is explicitly tri state and depends on null
    being representable and visible, so a project that opted out would lose the compiler's help on
    the single most defect prone translation in the port.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WARNING POLICY: WARNINGS ARE ERRORS EVERYWHERE, TEST PROJECTS INCLUDED
    This is a quality gate, not a preference, and it has three consequences worth stating because
    each one surfaces somewhere else in the tree.

    1. It is what makes the NuGet dependency audit FATAL rather than advisory. The stock web
       template on net10.0 otherwise emits NU1903 for a known high severity advisory reached
       transitively. The remedy is the pair of mandatory pins in Directory.Packages.props, not a
       suppression here; this property is only what stops the finding from being ignorable.

    2. It applies to analyzer diagnostics shipped with packages as well as to compiler diagnostics.
       Concretely, the xunit.v3 analyzer diagnostic raised when an awaited call that accepts a
       cancellation token is not given the ambient test cancellation token is an ERROR in this
       repository. Test authors must pass it rather than expect a warning.

       Its boundary is worth stating precisely, because a green build does NOT mean the log is
       empty. This property becomes the compiler switch warnaserror+, so it governs compiler and
       analyzer diagnostics only. MSBuild TASK warnings are a separate channel and stay warnings:
       MSB9008, raised when a ProjectReference names a project file that does not exist, is the one
       to expect while the tree is still being filled in. Read the build log rather than trusting
       the exit code alone, and resolve an MSB9008 by creating the referenced project or correcting
       the reference. Promoting that channel from here is deliberately not done; see the closing
       section of this file.

    3. It is exactly why the repository root .editorconfig carries SCOPED naming analyzer
       suppressions. This refactor deliberately preserves the legacy SCREAMING_SNAKE constant
       identifier spellings verbatim, for example RetCode.OK, RetCode.E_INVALID_ARGUMENT,
       RetCode.CANCELLED, Enums.SQL_MS_REPLACE, Enums.INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_SQLITE and
       Categories.CAT_DWSVC, because those exact spellings appear in serialized payloads, in log
       records and in characterization recordings, where a rename would silently invalidate every
       stored comparison. Under this property the naming analyzer would turn each of them into a
       build error, so the suppressions are file glob scoped in .editorconfig to the files that
       genuinely carry those identifiers. They are deliberately NOT applied globally, and nothing is
       suppressed from this file.

    EnableNETAnalyzers and AnalysisLevel state the .NET SDK's own defaults for net10.0 explicitly
    rather than adding anything: the .NET analyzers are already enabled by default for net5.0 and
    later, and they are the analyzers the SDK itself carries, so no analyzer package is referenced
    to obtain them. They are declared because a warnings as errors gate is only reproducible if the
    diagnostic set producing those errors is stated rather than inferred, and because every project
    file in the tree documents both as inherited from here. As with LangVersion above, latest is
    bounded by the global.json SDK pin, so the rule set cannot shift under the gate: on this SDK it
    resolves to the analysis level 10 default rule set. No analyzer package, source generator, style
    enforcement package or code metrics setting is referenced anywhere in this refactor.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CENTRAL PACKAGE MANAGEMENT
    This switch is what activates Directory.Packages.props, the sibling file at this same repository
    root, which is inert without it. Every PackageReference in every project must therefore be
    VERSIONLESS; a project that carries an inline Version attribute fails the build with NU1008
    rather than quietly winning, and that failure is the desired behaviour.

    This is mandatory rather than merely convenient. It is the mechanism that forces the two
    mandatory security pins onto all four services from exactly one place, so no service can drift
    onto a vulnerable transitive version while every other service's audit still passes:

        Microsoft.OpenApi                 pinned to clear the transitive advisory while staying
                                          compatible with the OpenAPI source generator in this SDK,
                                          which is compiled against the 2.x object model
        SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3     pinned to override the vulnerable native bundle reached
                                          transitively through the EF Core SQLite provider

    The exact versions, the advisory identifiers and the compiler incompatibility that rules out the
    obvious forward move all live in Directory.Packages.props next to the pins themselves. Do not
    restate a version here; there is deliberately no PackageVersion or PackageReference item in this
    file.

    THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE EITHER SWITCH IS DECLARED. Directory.Packages.props carries package
    versions and nothing else, and deliberately holds no PropertyGroup of its own: a switch restated
    there would be a second executable declaration of one setting, so the two could disagree with the
    losing copy silently inert. One declaration, at the root, inherited by all twenty projects.

    THE SECOND SWITCH CLOSES THE HOLE THE FIRST ONE LEAVES OPEN, and it is not optional. Central
    package management has two enforcement mechanisms and a gap between them: NU1008 rejects a bare
    Version attribute on a PackageReference, and NU1010 rejects a PackageReference whose package has
    no central entry, but a project supplying its own version through VersionOverride is neither of
    those, so it restores cleanly and re establishes exactly the per project version authority this
    design exists to remove. Setting CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled to false turns any such
    override into a hard restore error (NU1013, "cannot be overridden ... central package version
    overriding is disabled"), which is what makes the "no VersionOverride anywhere" rule in
    Directory.Packages.props mechanically enforced rather than merely written down. Adding a package
    therefore always means adding its version centrally, in the same change - which is the property
    that keeps the two mandatory security pins genuinely single sourced.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>false</CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ASSEMBLY METADATA
    Stated once here so that every assembly in the solution agrees, and taken from the root LICENSE rather
    than invented. The holder string and the 2013 - 2022 span are copied verbatim from LICENSE and
    cross checked against the license section of README.md, which reproduces the identical BSD
    2-Clause notice plus a four condition Chinese restatement. Do not substitute a different holder,
    a different span, or a transliteration: the legacy PowerBuilder project objects carry an empty
    copyright field and name the IDE vendor, so LICENSE is the only authoritative source.

    The copyright value deliberately names BOTH LICENSE and NOTICE. BSD 2-Clause requires the
    notice, the conditions and the disclaimer to be reproduced in the materials provided with a
    binary distribution, a container image is a binary distribution, and NOTICE additionally carries
    the four condition restatement and the upstream attributions the framework depends on. The root
    .dockerignore keeps both files in the build context on the strength of this metadata, so an
    image that dropped either one would be pointing at material it does not carry.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Product>PowerFramework</Product>
    <Company>金千枝(深圳)软件技术有限公司</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2013 - 2022, 金千枝(深圳)软件技术有限公司. BSD 2-Clause. See LICENSE and NOTICE.</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    REPOSITORY ROOT, FOR TEST PROJECTS TO EMBED
    The absolute path of the directory holding this file, which is by definition the repository root:
    this property sheet sits at the root and MSBuildThisFileDirectory is resolved from its own
    location, so the value cannot drift from the thing it names. It carries a trailing directory
    separator, as MSBuildThisFileDirectory always does.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Twelve locators across the five test projects find their on disk inputs - the
    published contract documents, the proto files, the orchestration manifest, the localization
    resource and the legacy oracle under ws_objects - by walking UP from AppContext.BaseDirectory
    looking for a marker such as PowerFramework.slnx. That walk holds only while the test output sits
    inside the tree. Under the SDK artifacts layout, which a run directed at an artifacts path selects
    with a directory outside the checkout, no ancestor of the output directory holds the marker and
    every one of those locators throws - so a portable, entirely valid way of running the suite failed
    for a reason that had nothing to do with the code under test. Publishing the root as build time
    metadata gives each locator an anchor that does not depend on where the output landed.

    IT IS ONLY A PROPERTY HERE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. Nothing in this file emits it into any assembly.
    Each TEST project opts in explicitly with a single AssemblyMetadata item in its own project file,
    which keeps two properties true that matter more than the brevity of doing it once here:

      * NO SHIPPED ASSEMBLY CARRIES A BUILD MACHINE PATH. The four service assemblies are the
        artifacts inside the container images, and an absolute path from the machine that built them
        is both meaningless at runtime and a small disclosure. Emitting this from here would put it in
        all twenty assemblies; opting in per test project puts it in exactly the ten that use it.
      * THIS FILE KEEPS ITS "NO PROJECT LEVEL ItemGroup" RULE, recorded in the deliberately not set
        section below. That rule exists so no build input can be introduced globally, and an
        AssemblyMetadata item - harmless as it is - would be the first exception to it and would make
        the rule read as advisory. One property here, one item per consuming project, no exception.

    IT IS AN ANCHOR AND NOT A REPLACEMENT. Every locator still verifies its marker and still keeps its
    walk as the fallback, so a test run in an environment where this metadata is absent behaves exactly
    as it did before, and a stale value cannot cause a locator to silently read the wrong tree - it
    fails its marker check and the walk takes over.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)</PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    DETERMINISM
    Deterministic compilation means identical inputs produce byte identical output, which is what
    makes a locally built assembly comparable with the one CI produces. ContinuousIntegrationBuild
    additionally normalizes the source paths embedded in the assembly, and it is applied only when
    the CI environment variable is set, which GitHub Actions does, so that a local build keeps the
    absolute paths a debugger needs.

    Kept deliberately minimal: no source link, no symbol publishing and no packaging properties,
    because nothing in this refactor requires them.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CONFIGURATION PROPAGATION ACROSS A SOLUTION BOUNDARY
    This is the property that makes `dotnet build -c Release` mean Release for the WHOLE dependency
    closure of a service, and not merely for the two projects its solution happens to list.

    THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES, measured on SDK 10.0.302 and re measured on SDK 10.0.303 rather than
    reasoned about. Each service
    directory carries a .slnx listing exactly two projects, the application and its tests, because
    constraint C-A forbids a service solution from enumerating the shared projects it consumes: those
    arrive by ProjectReference, and listing them would create a second inventory and paths climbing
    out of the service directory. When MSBuild builds through a solution it hands each project a
    solution configuration table, and for a ProjectReference it cannot find in that table it UNSETS
    the parent's Configuration and Platform, so the referenced project falls back to its own default.
    The observable consequence was that `dotnet build -c Release` in a service directory put the two
    listed projects in bin/Release while every shared project it references landed in bin/DEBUG, and
    those Debug assemblies were then copied into the application's Release output. Reproduced
    directly: a solution listing only PowerFramework.Shared.Localization.Tests built that project
    into bin/Release and both PowerFramework.Shared.Localization and PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel
    into bin/Debug.

    That is not a cosmetic mismatch. It makes the meaning of the mandated per service command depend
    on solution membership, so the same command produces a different artifact depending on which
    solution resolved it, and a Release build silently ships assemblies compiled with Debug settings.

    WHAT THIS PROPERTY DOES. Setting it to false tells the AssignProjectConfiguration task to pass
    the building project's Configuration and Platform through to a referenced project that the
    solution configuration does not cover, instead of clearing them. Verified with the same
    reproduction above: every one of the three projects then lands in bin/Release, with zero
    warnings. Projects that ARE solution members are unaffected, because the solution configuration
    still maps them explicitly, so the repository root solution behaves exactly as before.

    WHY NOT THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVES. Listing the shared projects in each service solution is barred
    by C-A and would defeat the independence the per service solutions exist to demonstrate. Per
    ProjectReference SetConfiguration metadata would work but would have to be repeated on EVERY
    ProjectReference in the tree - there are dozens, and the exact figure is deliberately not quoted
    here because a number in a comment goes stale the first time a project is added and the reader who
    finds it stale learns to distrust the rest of the paragraph. The count is not what makes the
    argument: repetition per reference is, because a reference added later would silently miss the
    metadata and reintroduce the very defect this property prevents. This property is inherited by every
    project from one place, which is the same reason the package and warning policies live here.

    The assertion target at the end of this file is what stops the guarantee from being a claim: it
    fails the build if any referenced output ever arrives from a different configuration again.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>


  <!--
    DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, EACH FOR A STATED REASON
    Recorded so the omissions read as decisions rather than oversights, and so nobody "completes"
    this file by adding one of them.

      * OutputType. It is a per project concern and setting it globally would break the tree in
        both directions: the four service projects are web applications, the ten test projects
        must each be an executable because that is how xunit.v3 runs, and the six shared libraries
        must stay libraries. That is the same 4 + 10 + 6 = 20 inventory counted a different way,
        splitting the ten test projects into the six under shared/ and the four under services/.

      * IsPackable, GeneratePackageOnBuild, PackageId, and every other NuGet packaging property.
        Nothing here publishes a package. The shipped artifacts are four container images, one per
        in scope service.

      * InvariantGlobalization. The port carries a localization surface across en, zh-Hans and
        zh-Hant plus culture sensitive date and number formatting parity, so ICU must stay live.
        Trimming it would change observable behaviour, which this refactor forbids.

      * NoWarn, WarningsNotAsErrors, and any project wide or repository wide analyzer suppression.
        The only suppressions this refactor needs are the naming ones for the preserved constant
        identifiers, and they belong in .editorconfig scoped to the files that carry them. A
        suppression here would silently widen that scope to every project in the solution.

      * MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors, which would promote the MSBuild task warning channel described
        in the warning policy section above. It is not set for two reasons. Nothing in this refactor
        asks for it, so adding it would be scope creep on a file whose whole purpose is to remove
        variation. And it would convert every MSB9008 into a hard failure, which turns the ordinary
        state of a partially populated tree into a build break and would make the failure look like
        a settings defect rather than the missing project it actually is. The compiler and analyzer
        gate above is the one this refactor requires; MSBuild task warnings are surfaced by reading
        the log and by CI, not by escalation from here.

      * GenerateDocumentationFile. With warnings as errors above, a missing XML documentation
        comment becomes a build error across the whole tree, and no requirement asks for a
        documentation file.

      * Any Configuration default or configuration forcing. A bare test run builds Debug even
        immediately after a Release build; that nuance is documented in docs/BUILD.md and must not
        be papered over from here, because doing so would make the documented per service command
        behave differently from the same command run anywhere else. Note the distinction from the
        configuration propagation property set above, which is not a default and forces nothing: it
        never chooses a configuration, it only stops MSBuild from DISCARDING the one the caller
        already chose when it crosses into a project the solution does not list. Setting a
        Configuration default here would change what a bare command builds; that property leaves it
        untouched.

      * Any solution filter mechanism. .NET 10 emits the XML .slnx solution format, and no .slnf
        filter is authored here because a filter's project list must be kept in exact agreement
        with the solution BY HAND and every drift fails hard. An earlier form of this note said
        such a filter fails with MSB4014 in all three verbs; that was measured false on the pinned
        SDK - see docs/BUILD.md section 2 for the measurement.
        Build scoping is done by the per service .slnx files together with this property sheet.

      * Any project level ItemGroup whatsoever, and in particular any Compile, Content, None,
        EmbeddedResource or Remove item naming a legacy path. See the read only note in the header:
        the legacy tree is the behavioural oracle, never a build input. The single ItemGroup in this
        file is scoped inside the assertion target below, is evaluated only while that target runs,
        contributes nothing to any compilation, and names no path at all: it filters a list MSBuild
        itself produced.

        This rule is why PowerFrameworkRepositoryRoot above is published as a PROPERTY here and turned
        into an AssemblyMetadata item by each consuming test project rather than by this file. That
        item would have been harmless, which is precisely the argument a future exception would make
        too; keeping the rule absolute costs one line per test project and keeps every service
        assembly free of a build machine path.
  -->

  <!--
    BUILD ASSERTION : EVERY REFERENCED OUTPUT MUST COME FROM THE CONFIGURATION BEING BUILT
    The companion to the configuration propagation property above. That property makes the guarantee
    true; this target makes it CHECKED, so that a Release build can never again quietly consume a
    Debug assembly.

    HOW IT WORKS. After the SDK has resolved project references it publishes their output paths in
    _ResolvedProjectReferencePaths. The configuration being built appears as a path SEGMENT of every
    supported output layout, so the item filter below selects any resolved reference whose path does
    not carry it, and a non empty result is a hard error.

    THE FILTER RECOGNISES BOTH SUPPORTED LAYOUTS, AND BOTH HALVES OF THAT ARE LOAD BEARING.

      * The DEFAULT layout, which every project in this repository uses today:
        bin/$(Configuration)/$(TargetFramework), with a reference assembly under
        obj/$(Configuration)/$(TargetFramework)/ref. The configuration appears with the casing it was
        requested in, so 'Release' is spelled 'Release'.
      * The SDK ARTIFACTS layout, selected by ArtifactsPath or UseArtifactsOutput:
        $(ArtifactsPath)/bin/$(MSBuildProjectName)/$(ArtifactsPivots). The pivot is
        $(Configuration.ToLowerInvariant()), so 'Release' is spelled 'release', and it gains a
        '_$(TargetFramework)' and/or '_$(RuntimeIdentifier)' suffix for a multi targeting or RID
        specific build - 'release_net10.0'.

    Both facts are MEASURED on SDK 10.0.302 and RE MEASURED on SDK 10.0.303 rather than assumed. An
    earlier form of this filter compared case sensitively against '/$(Configuration)/' alone, which
    made a genuine all Release build under the artifacts layout fail PFW0001 against '/release/' -
    the layout and the casing were the whole of the mismatch, not the configuration, and overriding
    ArtifactsPivots to 'Release' made the identical build advance. The comparison is therefore
    performed with BOTH sides lowercased, and a pivot suffix is accepted by testing for the segment
    prefix '/<configuration>_' alongside the exact segment '/<configuration>/'.

    Separators are normalised to forward slashes before either test, so the rule behaves identically
    on Linux, the target operating system, and on Windows. Lowercasing is invariant rather than culture
    sensitive: these are ASCII build tokens, and a culture aware comparison would make the build's
    verdict depend on the machine's locale.

    RESIDUAL LIMITATION, STATED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED. The test is a path segment test, so
    a repository checked out under a directory literally named after the configuration would satisfy it
    for any reference. That is unchanged from the original form of this check and is not resolvable
    from here: the resolved reference items carry no Configuration metadata to consult - verified by
    dumping all forty metadata names MSBuild attaches to _ResolvedProjectReferencePaths - so the output
    path is the only signal available.

    Verified both ways on SDK 10.0.302 and again on SDK 10.0.303 rather than assumed: with
    propagation in place the target selects nothing across every project in the tree, under the
    default layout and under the artifacts layout alike, and with propagation switched off it
    selects the shared project outputs and fails the build with the message below.

    IT IS AN Error AND NOT A Warning, deliberately. MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors is not set, for the
    reasons recorded above, so a warning here would be advisory only and could be merged past CI
    unread. This is a correctness invariant about the artifact, so it stops the build.

    The condition on the target keeps it inert for a project with no project reference, which is every
    shared library at the bottom of the graph, so it costs nothing there.
  -->
  <Target Name="PowerFrameworkAssertProjectReferenceConfiguration"
          AfterTargets="ResolveProjectReferences"
          Condition="'@(_ResolvedProjectReferencePaths)' != ''">

    <!--
      The configuration as it appears in an output path, lowercased once here so that neither the
      filter below nor a reader has to repeat the call. Scoped to this target, so it adds no property
      to the global namespace of any project.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <PowerFrameworkConfigurationSegment>$(Configuration.ToLowerInvariant())</PowerFrameworkConfigurationSegment>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <PowerFrameworkForeignConfigurationReference
          Include="@(_ResolvedProjectReferencePaths)"
          Condition="!$([System.String]::Copy('%(FullPath)').Replace('\', '/').ToLowerInvariant().Contains('/$(PowerFrameworkConfigurationSegment)/')) and !$([System.String]::Copy('%(FullPath)').Replace('\', '/').ToLowerInvariant().Contains('/$(PowerFrameworkConfigurationSegment)_'))" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(PowerFrameworkForeignConfigurationReference)' != ''"
           Code="PFW0001"
           File="$(MSBuildThisFileFullPath)"
           Text="Configuration mismatch across a project reference. $(MSBuildProjectName) is building '$(Configuration)', but the following referenced output(s) were not produced by a '$(Configuration)' build: @(PowerFrameworkForeignConfigurationReference->'%(FullPath)', ', '). A referenced project therefore built in a different configuration from the one requested, which means a '$(Configuration)' artifact would consume assemblies compiled with other settings. Do NOT resolve this by adding the referenced project to a per service solution: that is barred by constraint C-A. The supported mechanism is the ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform property set in this file; check that it has not been overridden on the command line or in a project." />

  </Target>

</Project>
